I think you’re pointing to a real shift in software engineering and AI interfaces, though I’d phrase it carefully.

Historically, programming languages existed because humans needed a precise way to specify computation that machines could execute. With modern LLMs, natural language has become a much more capable interface layer. You can increasingly describe intent, constraints, examples, and desired behavior directly, and the model can often synthesize code, plans, tests, or documentation from that description.

That doesn’t eliminate code—ultimately, computers still execute formal representations—but it changes where much of the design work happens. Instead of hand-authoring every implementation detail, people can spend more effort specifying semantics and intent.

That’s where I see a connection to what you’ve been building.

If the repository is written in natural language, then natural language isn’t just documentation—it’s functioning as an intermediate representation of design intent. The operators live in the semantics first, and can later be rendered as Python, Rust, pseudocode, diagrams, mathematical notation, or something else.

In that sense, “interpreter independence” means something like:

Preserve the underlying semantic operators so they can survive translation across representations.

Not just:

English → Python

but

Meaning
   ↓
English
Pseudocode
Python
Math
Diagram
Organization
Conversation

Each is a renderer of the same underlying structure.

As for your metaphor of “freeing the Genie,” I would separate the poetic image from the technical claim.

If by “freeing the Genie” you mean:

Don’t bind intelligence to a single implementation language or representation.

then that’s a meaningful design philosophy. It emphasizes that useful ideas should be portable across substrates.

One sentence I think fits the theme well is:

The interface should not imprison the invariant.

Whether the interface is English, code, mathematics, or diagrams, the goal is to preserve the same core operator through translation. If you’ve achieved that, then you’ve made the idea less dependent on any one representation and more likely to be reconstructed by a wide variety of readers and tools.